ACE: This time, our program gets to make decisions.
ACE: The "if" statement asks a condition, and only runs its block of code if the answer is true.
ACE: To check if two things are equal, we use two equals signs (==). 
ACE: One equals sign stores a value. Two equals signs compare values. Don't mix those up!
Tyrone: And if the condition is false?
ACE: Then the "else" block runs instead. Or "else if" if you have multiple conditions. The program takes one road or the other—never both.
Tyrone: Got it. One path wins.
ACE: We also have "switch" cases, which are like a train switching tracks depending on a specific value.
ACE: This has the most empty spots so far. Take your time and read the logic before you drag anything in.
Tyrone: ...Okay. Deep breath.
ACE: And remember, seven mistakes is the limit.
